Falsifies `machine_discovery::framebuffer::verification::an_encoded_token_never_exceeds_the_maximum_it_advertises`.

The defect: `MAX_LEN` drops to 62, one below the encoder's real worst case of 63.

This is not an invented mistake, it is the one the constant's own doc records
happening. `MAX_LEN` is "rounded up rather than derived", so nothing ties it to
what `encode` writes: the token was `fb=` when 64 was chosen, calef ratified the
four-characters-longer `screen=` on 2026-09-04, and the worst case went to 63 of
64 with nothing noticing. One byte of headroom is what the doc calls "a trap for
whoever adds a field".

The failure is sharp because the constant is what every caller sizes its buffer
by: the harness allocates `[u8; MAX_LEN]` exactly as the UEFI loader does, so an
encoder that writes past the advertised maximum writes past the buffer. The
round-trip test in this file cannot catch it, because its descriptions are
realistic geometries whose tokens are far shorter than the worst case.

Expected to fail: the bounds check inside `encode`'s `put` closure, on a base near
`u64::MAX` with three ten-digit fields.

diff --git a/crates/machine_discovery/src/framebuffer.rs b/crates/machine_discovery/src/framebuffer.rs
--- a/crates/machine_discovery/src/framebuffer.rs
+++ b/crates/machine_discovery/src/framebuffer.rs
@@ -84,7 +84,7 @@ impl Framebuffer {
     /// The worst case is 63 bytes: `screen=`, `0x` and sixteen hex digits, three ten-digit decimals
     /// with their commas, and a four-character order. Rounded up with room to spare, because a
     /// token that overflows its own buffer fails between two programs, where it is hardest to see.
-    pub const MAX_LEN: usize = 72;
+    pub const MAX_LEN: usize = 62;
 
     /// How many bytes the console may touch: `stride * height`.
     ///
